+directory 0700), see tudor_pairing_* in tudor.c and tudor_tls.c.
+
+Rules the driver follows: the directory must be writable before the sensor
+is paired, a failed save fails the open. The stored pairing is only given
+up when the handshake ends in a plaintext fatal alert about the certificate
+(handshake_failure, bad_certificate and relatives, never seen so far), the
+file then moves to `<id>.pairing.old` and the sensor is paired again with a
+warning. A garbled reply, a bad tag or a timeout never touch the file. A
+first pairing of a sensor that was paired before (Windows, a lost file)
+makes its templates unusable and is only logged as a warning.
+
+Upstream question, for the issue: libfprint has no per-device persistent
+data API, and no driver touches the file system. Options: (a) a small core
+API for driver state with the directory from STATE_DIRECTORY (fprintd's unit
+sets it) or a meson option, (b) a driver-local file under a meson
+`-Dstate_dir`, with the env override allowed only in emulation mode (the env
+var is a development aid here), (c) derive the host key from the sensor id
+and a built-in secret, which removes the file but makes the pairing public.
+
+Emulation mode (FP_DEVICE_EMULATION, tests only): no pairing file is read
+or written, all randomness comes from a fixed seed (tudor_tls_set_seed),
+ECDSA uses RFC 6979 nonces, so a recording replays byte for byte. The
+recording itself needs a sensor that is paired from scratch, see
+tests/synaptics_tudor/README.md.
+
+After the template list the Windows driver lists the payloads of a
+template (`9f 03 00 00 00 <template id>`), asks object info (`a0 03 00 00
+00 <payload id>`) and reads the data (`a1 03 00 00 00 <payload id>`). List
+does the same, see "Storage". On a sensor with 3 templates only the one
+enrolled by the Windows driver had a payload below it.
 
