 ### Storage and the print mapping
 
-The sensor only gives the payload back inside an identify reply, so the
-template to print mapping (username, finger) cannot be listed without a
-finger. Nothing in the trace reads an object's data (0xa1 in proto.txt).
-The driver treats the template id as the print: prints carry the id as
-data, verify and identify compare the id the sensor reports to the one in
-the print, list gives one print per template without username or finger.
+DB info counts users, templates and payloads as current, deleted and
+available. A delete only raises "deleted", nothing is reclaimed until the
+cleanup `a4 <n>` runs, n being the object type (1 users, 2 templates, 3
+payloads). The vendor driver runs all three right after a delete. Without
+it a sensor runs dry: the owner's sensor sat at 1 template, 10 deleted, 0
+available. The driver checks DB info before an enroll, reclaims when
+something deleted is there and otherwise fails with DATA_FULL, and does
+the cleanup after each delete. Cleanup is untested on hardware until the
+owner session ran.
+
+The reply to `a1 03 00 00 00 <payload id>` is a reserved u16, the u32
+length and then the list of entries the commit had (template id, identity,
+finger, payload). Prints carry the template id as data, verify and
+identify compare the id the sensor reports to it. List reads the payload of
+each template to fill in finger and, if the identity carries one, the user
+id string.
 
